WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:13.420
[Music]

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:18.860
[Narrator:] Controlling flies effectively depends upon adapting the control methods to fly habits.

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:24.580
These habits will vary with the changing conditions of weather, temperature, and season of the year.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:32.700
Therefore the control operator must have a knowledge of fly biology to apply his measures to the right place at the right time.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:41.740
Typical of most of the higher insects, the lifecycle of the housefly starts with an egg from which comes the maggot or larva.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:51.700
The larva forms a pupa similar to the chrysalis of a butterfly and from the pupa emerges the adult fly.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:01:01.760
The eggs are laid in batches on decaying organic matter and a single housefly may lay well over 500 eggs in her lifetime.

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:06.480
Under normal summertime conditions the eggs will hatch in 24 hours or less.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:15.060
The larvae feed voraciously and pass through several growth stages or instars.

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:21.820
When full-grown they stop feeding, move to a drier part of the medium, and become immobilized.

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:28.880
Their shape changes and the outer skin hardens, becoming reddish-brown or black.

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:34.840
This is the pupal case in which the immature insect changes into the adult fly.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:43.180
To escape, the fly breaks the pupal case by inflating the ptilinum, a bladder-like organ on the front of the head.

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:55.100
When the fly first emerges, the wings are small and the ptilinum extends prominently forward.

00:01:55.900 --> 00:02:04.600
But soon the ptilinum disappears and the wings expand to full-size. The fly is now completely developed and will not grow any larger.

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:12.600
The total period from egg to adult is eight to 20 days and there may be 15 generations a year.

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:17.660
When we talk about domestic flies we should include several relatives of the housefly.

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:26.720
First there are the blowflies, generally larger than the housefly and usually shiny metallic blue or green.

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:31.460
When blowfly larvae attain full growth they leave the breeding medium

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:37.460
and may crawl for several yards before burrowing into the soil to pupate.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:47.740
Another domestic fly is the flesh fly, typically with a checkerboard pattern on the upper side of the abdomen.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:55.380
Similar in appearance to the housefly is the stable fly. The chief difference is in the mouth parts.

00:02:56.100 --> 00:03:01.680
The housefly's mouth parts extend downward and end in a spongy pad called the labellum.

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:10.640
Unlike the housefly, the stable fly is a blood-feeder, and its mouth parts project straight forward like a bayonet.

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:18.580
Flies are capable of a considerable flight range. Blowflies ordinarily cover a wider range than houseflies,

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:23.520
but under certain conditions both types will cover a distance of 10 miles or more.

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:30.800
This means that breeding sites some distance away may be the source of flies in the community.

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:35.560
The term breeding sites includes almost any decaying organic material

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:39.840
offering the necessary food and moisture for larval development.

00:03:40.040 --> 00:03:44.600
In the fringe area these will include stock farms,

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:47.700
vegetable processing plants,

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:51.280
and especially open garbage dumps on the edge of the community.

00:03:52.700 --> 00:04:00.620
When a fly population builds up to this extent, overcrowding may drive them to the nearby town in search of new breeding sites.

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:06.217
A brief survey of the average community will reveal many sources of flies:

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:15.320
household garbage, industrial waste. Here maggots are concentrated in the runoff from a rendering plant.

00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:21.520
Food waste from markets and restaurants and exposed animal carcasses.

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:26.540
This contact of flies with filth and bacteria makes them a menace to public health.

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:33.140
Adult flies are unable to eat solid food. They feed only on the liquid part of the rotting matter.

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:37.540
Then when they come into our homes to enjoy a bit of sugar, they first dissolve it

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:44.460
by regurgitating some of the same contaminated fluid into the sugar bowl.

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:48.340
But perhaps even more important are the familiar fly specks or germ-laden excreta.

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:54.820
Certainly there can be no doubt of the ability of flies to transport filth and disease organisms.

00:04:56.500 --> 00:05:01.100
Removal of the breeding sources is the most effective measure of all in cutting down the fly population.

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:08.580
That is basic sanitation, a primary objective in community fly control.

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:17.580
But the use of insecticides is an important supplementary measure

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:22.820
and here a knowledge of adult fly habits is absolutely necessary.

00:05:22.820 --> 00:05:26.880
Flies will not always be found where we expect them.

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:30.220
For instance, they usually remain close to sources of food

00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:33.800
but at the approach of a storm houseflies will leave these attractants

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:40.520
and gather at door or window screens in their attempt to find shelter indoors.

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:44.760
During the cool morning hours flies will rest on walls and fences in the sun.

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:55.540
But in the heat of a summer day the same flies will gather at cool moist places, such as this damp spot in a dirt road.

00:05:56.120 --> 00:06:02.660
Consideration of these changing daytime habits of flies is the key to effective space-spraying.

00:06:03.060 --> 00:06:12.700
At night flies are inactive, blowflies resting outdoors on wires, low branches, or more commonly on foliage.

00:06:12.920 --> 00:06:17.400
Houseflies, on the other hand, normally rest indoors at night.

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:23.151
By daylight the control operator can recognize the preferred night resting places

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:27.900
by the concentrations of fly specks along cracks in walls,

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:36.840
on overhead electric wires, or on ceilings and rafters, usually preferring the edges of boards and beams.

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:44.660
Control experts make use of this knowledge by examining walls and ceilings for indications of preferred night-resting places.

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:55.480
These areas are then sprayed with residual insecticides, for here the flies will remain all night in contact with the poison.

00:06:55.860 --> 00:07:03.540
However, residual insecticides are also effective where flies regularly gather in the daytime.

00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:12.520
The recent widespread use of chemicals for fly control has brought to light another very important phase of fly biology.

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:18.640
Early in a fly-control program, applications of the insecticide give encouraging results.

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:25.520
But then the flies begin to increase in numbers and further use of the insecticide has little effect.

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:30.800
In short, the flies have become resistant to the spray material.

00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:37.420
All of these facts make it apparent that fly habits do not conform to a fixed pattern.

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:42.000
That is why the control program cannot be reduced to a formula.

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:45.760
The successful operator must know fly biology.

00:07:46.040 --> 00:07:56.680
He must know that the entire lifecycle of the fly--egg to larva to pupa to adult--takes place in or near the source of larval food.

00:07:57.020 --> 00:08:05.400
He should suspect any accumulation material as being a possible source of flies, a source that should be eliminated completely.

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:12.640
Daytime conditions of weather and temperature will dictate where and when to space-spray,

00:08:12.700 --> 00:08:16.460
while the location of fly specks will indicate nighttime resting places

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:22.560
areas where the application of residual insecticides will give maximum results.

00:08:23.080 --> 00:08:32.017
Consideration of these phases of fly biology means more efficient, more complete control of domestic flies.

00:08:32.017 --> 00:08:39.817
[Music]

